---
# Slice 2 of the ingress default-deny. The baseline (roles/k8s/netpol-baseline) admits traefik,
# prometheus and the node bridges to every pod carrying `netpol-baseline: enforced`. This file adds
# sonarr's own callers, which are all direct pod → ClusterIP and therefore invisible to the
# baseline.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED, and for the callers below that scoping is the whole grant: the baseline
# admits none of janitorr, configarr, monitor-bridge, autofix-bridge, bazarr or prowlarr, so they
# reach sonarr only through this rule and only on this one port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS LISTED HERE DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant — but be accurate about why.
# The baseline admits traefik to every baseline-labelled pod on ANY port, and policies are
# additive, so while sonarr carries that label the union is the same with or without this entry.
# It was load-bearing for the deploy that INTRODUCED the label: the old pod carried `app: sonarr`
# without `netpol-baseline: enforced`, so this policy was the only one selecting it and the UI
# would have 502'd for the length of that rollout. That window is closed — every pod created from
# here on carries both labels. It stays because this policy then states its own complete caller set
# rather than depending on the baseline continuing to admit traefik on all ports: if that baseline
# rule is ever port-scoped, or this pod template loses the label, this line is what keeps the UI up.
#
# bazarr addresses sonarr by BARE NAME from its own application database, not from any manifest
# (roles/k8s/bazarr/defaults/main.yml). It is in this list because of that comment, not because a
# grep found it — dropping it would break subtitle fetching silently.
#
# prowlarr is here for the REVERSE of the sonarr→prowlarr direction documented elsewhere: its
# Application Sync POSTs indexer definitions INTO sonarr over the ClusterIP
# (`[POST] http://sonarr:8989/api/v3/indexer?forceSave=true`, seen live in prowlarr's logs).
# Same class as bazarr above — configured in prowlarr's own application database on its PVC, not
# in any manifest here — so it is invisible to grep and easy to mistake for an unused caller.
# Fenced, sonarr silently keeps stale indexer definitions and nothing alerts, because
# monitor-bridge's "Prowlarr Indexers" check reads prowlarr's own /api/v1/indexerstatus, which
# this breakage does not touch.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: sonarr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: sonarr
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
        - podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                  - janitorr        # templates/config/application.yml.j2 — deploy-time verify too
                  - configarr       # CronJob; its pod template carries app: configarr
                  - monitor-bridge  # env-secret.yaml.j2, cluster FQDN
                  - autofix-bridge  # env-secret.yaml.j2, cluster FQDN
                  - bazarr          # app DB, bare name — see header
                  - prowlarr        # Application Sync writes indexers in — see header
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
